-- Q2 [Bone Structure & Marrow] medium skill1 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Bone Structure & Marrow', 'A patient with severe anemia is referred for a bone-marrow biopsy to evaluate the production of new red blood cells. To assess hematopoiesis, the physician should sample which tissue?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Yellow marrow of the medullary cavity"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Red marrow of the spongy bone"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Compact bone of the diaphysis"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Hyaline cartilage of the epiphyseal plate"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because red marrow is the tissue where hematopoiesis—the production of blood cells—takes place. In bone, red marrow fills the spaces within the spongy bone of the epiphysis, and the textbook states that red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets are all produced in the red marrow. To evaluate the production of new red blood cells, a physician therefore samples red marrow, the only marrow type that performs blood-cell production. Yellow marrow, by contrast, contains adipose tissue and stores triglycerides as an energy reserve rather than producing blood cells. (Choice A) Yellow marrow fills the medullary cavity of the diaphysis and contains adipose tissue that stores triglycerides for energy; it does not perform hematopoiesis, so sampling it would not assess red blood cell production. (Choice B) is correct: red marrow fills the spaces in the spongy bone of the epiphysis and is the site of hematopoiesis, where red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets are produced, making it the tissue that must be sampled. (Choice C) Compact bone is the dense, hard tissue forming the walls of the diaphysis; it provides structural strength and is not a marrow tissue, so it does not produce blood cells. (Choice D) Hyaline cartilage of the epiphyseal plate is the growth-plate tissue responsible for lengthwise bone growth in a growing bone, not a hematopoietic tissue. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that red marrow, not yellow marrow, is the bone tissue that carries out hematopoiesis.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 100)
  RETURNING id
)
