-- Q3 [Bone Structure & Marrow] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Bone Structure & Marrow', 'A pediatric femur fracture damages the hyaline cartilage of the distal epiphyseal plate, causing it to ossify into an epiphyseal line years ahead of the contralateral limb. Compared with the uninjured limb, the injured femur is most likely to show which outcome at skeletal maturity?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Greater final length from accelerated ossification"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Normal length once remodeling resumes after fusion"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Reduced final length from arrested lengthening"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Reduced final diameter from halted appositional growth"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because the epiphyseal plate is the sole site of longitudinal bone growth, so its premature ossification arrests lengthening and leaves the injured femur shorter than its uninjured pair. The epiphyseal plate is a layer of growing hyaline cartilage at the metaphysis that lengthens a long bone as its cartilage is replaced by bone added to the diaphysis; when the chondrocytes cease proliferating and bone replaces the cartilage, longitudinal growth stops and only the epiphyseal line remains. The textbook states that because cartilage is softer than bone, injury to a growing long bone can damage the epiphyseal plate cartilage, thus stopping bone growth and preventing additional bone lengthening. A plate that closes years early therefore stops adding length sooner than the normal contralateral plate, so the injured femur reaches skeletal maturity shorter overall. (Choice A) Ossification of the plate ends rather than accelerates growth, because once the cartilage is fully replaced by bone the plate becomes an inert epiphyseal line and no longer contributes length, so premature ossification cannot make the bone longer. (Choice B) Remodeling resorbs and renews existing bone on the same surface but does not regenerate the lost cartilage plate or restore lost length, so fusion permanently caps the bone''s length. (Choice C) is correct: with the growth plate prematurely converted to bone, the cellular division in the proliferative zone that drives lengthening ceases early, yielding a shorter final length than the intact limb. (Choice D) Diameter increases by appositional growth, in which osteoblasts deposit bone beneath the periosteum and this can continue even after longitudinal growth ceases, so loss of the plate does not halt widening. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the epiphyseal plate''s role in longitudinal growth to predict the skeletal consequence of its premature closure.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
