-- Q4 [Bone Structure & Marrow] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Bone Structure & Marrow', 'A surgeon realigns a patient''s proximal femur so that the dominant compressive load now passes through the femoral neck at a markedly different angle than before. Over the following months, the spongy bone of the femoral neck remodels in response to the new loading. Which change in the trabecular network is most likely?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Trabeculae fuse into concentric lamellae around central canals"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Trabeculae thicken uniformly in every direction"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Trabeculae dissolve as osteoclasts release stored calcium"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Trabeculae realign along the new lines of stress"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because each trabecula forms along the lines of mechanical stress, so a new loading direction drives the network to reorient toward those new stress lines. In spongy (cancellous) bone, the osteocytes sit in a lattice-like network of matrix spikes called trabeculae, and although the lattice may look random, each trabecula forms along lines of stress to provide strength to the bone (lines 9662-9664). Bone is not static: the internal and external structure of a bone changes as stress increases or decreases, and the bones undergo remodeling as a result of the forces placed on them (lines 10067-10072). Because the realignment redirects the dominant compressive load, the remodeling osteoblasts and osteoclasts rebuild the trabeculae oriented to the new stress lines while preserving the lightening open spaces of the network (line 9664). (Choice A) Concentric lamellae arranged around a central canal describe the osteon, the structural unit of compact bone, where the matrix is arranged in concentric rings (lines 9650-9651); spongy-bone osteocytes are explicitly not arranged in concentric circles (lines 9661-9662), so trabeculae do not convert into osteons. (Choice B) Uniform thickening in every direction ignores the central principle that trabeculae form along specific lines of stress (line 9664); remodeling is directional, matching bone geometry to the loading pattern rather than adding mass isotropically. (Choice C) Wholesale dissolution with calcium release describes osteoclast-driven resorption serving calcium homeostasis, as when parathyroid hormone releases calcium from bone into the circulation (line 10220); a redirected mechanical load remodels and reorients trabeculae rather than eliminating them, since mechanical stress stimulates deposition of mineral salts (line 10067). (Choice D) is correct: because each trabecula forms along lines of stress (line 9664) and the structure of a bone changes with the forces placed on it (lines 10068-10072), redirecting the compressive load reorients the trabeculae to align with the new stress lines. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that trabeculae form along lines of stress to predict how the network reorganizes under an altered loading scenario.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
