-- Q6 [Bone Matrix & Microstructure] easy skill1 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Bone Matrix & Microstructure', 'A histologist examines a cross-section of the dense compact bone forming the shaft of a femur and identifies the repeating structural unit from which this tissue is built. Which structure does the histologist identify?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A trabecula"}, {"label": "B", "text": "An osteon"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A single lamella"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A central canal"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because the osteon, or Haversian system, is the microscopic structural unit from which compact bone is built. Compact bone is organized into repeating osteons, each composed of concentric rings of calcified matrix called lamellae surrounding a central (Haversian) canal that carries blood vessels, nerves, and lymphatic vessels (OpenStax A&P 2e, lines 9650-9652). The lamellae and central canal are components within an osteon, not the unit itself, and trabeculae belong to spongy bone rather than compact bone. (Choice A) A trabecula is incorrect: trabeculae are the lattice-like matrix spikes that make up spongy (cancellous) bone, where lacunae are not arranged in concentric circles, so a trabecula is the structural element of spongy bone rather than compact bone (lines 9661-9663). (Choice B) is correct: the osteon (Haversian system) is the basic structural unit of compact bone, made of concentric layers of calcified matrix (lines 9650, 10364-10366). (Choice C) A single lamella is incorrect: lamellae are the concentric rings of calcified matrix that compose each osteon, so a lamella is one component within the unit, not the repeating unit itself (line 9651). (Choice D) A central canal is incorrect: the central (Haversian) canal runs down the center of each osteon and houses vessels and nerves, making it an internal feature of the unit rather than the unit as a whole (lines 9651-9652). This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that the osteon is the defined structural unit of compact bone.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
