-- Q7 [Bone Matrix & Microstructure] medium skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Bone Matrix & Microstructure', 'A histologist examines a stained cross-section of compact bone and locates the cell bodies of the osteocytes embedded at the borders of adjacent concentric lamellae within an osteon. The small spaces that directly enclose these osteocyte cell bodies are the:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "central canals"}, {"label": "B", "text": "canaliculi"}, {"label": "C", "text": "lacunae"}, {"label": "D", "text": "lamellae"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because each osteocyte cell body sits within a small space called a lacuna, located at the borders of adjacent lamellae. In compact bone, osteons are built from concentric rings of calcified matrix called lamellae, and the osteocytes are located inside spaces called lacunae found at the borders of adjacent lamellae (OpenStax A&P 2e, lines 9656–9658). Each osteocyte is housed in one lacuna and surrounded by bone tissue (line 9580); the cell maintains the surrounding matrix and exchanges nutrients and wastes through thin cytoplasmic processes that radiate out from the lacuna. (Choice A) Central (Haversian) canals run down the core of each osteon and contain blood vessels, nerves, and lymphatic vessels, not the osteocyte cell bodies (lines 9651–9652), so they supply the osteon rather than house its cells. (Choice B) Canaliculi are channels within the bone matrix that house the osteocyte''s cytoplasmic processes and connect lacunae to one another and to the central canal (lines 9582–9583, 9657–9658); they carry the processes for nutrient and waste exchange but do not enclose the cell body itself. (Choice C) is correct: the osteocytes are located inside spaces called lacunae, found at the borders of adjacent lamellae (lines 9656–9657), making the lacuna the structure that directly houses each osteocyte. (Choice D) Lamellae are the concentric rings of calcified matrix that make up an osteon (lines 9650–9651); the lacunae lie at the borders between adjacent lamellae, so lamellae form the surrounding matrix rather than the space that holds the cell. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall which named microstructure of bone directly encloses the osteocyte cell body.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 100)
  RETURNING id
)
