-- Q8 [Bone Matrix & Microstructure] medium skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Bone Matrix & Microstructure', 'In a section of compact bone, a histologist traces a blood vessel that travels transversely, at right angles to the long axis of the diaphysis, to connect the vascular core of one osteon with that of an adjacent osteon. This vessel is most likely passing through which structure?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A perforating (Volkmann''s) canal"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A central (Haversian) canal"}, {"label": "C", "text": "A canaliculus"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A lacuna"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because perforating (Volkmann''s) canals run transversely, branching off from the central canals to carry vessels and nerves between osteons and out to the periosteum and endosteum. In compact bone, each osteon has a central (Haversian) canal running longitudinally down its core, carrying blood vessels, nerves, and lymphatic vessels. The textbook states that these vessels and nerves "branch off at right angles through a perforating canal, also known as Volkmann''s canals, to extend to the periosteum and endosteum," so a vessel running transversely between adjacent osteons travels through a perforating canal. (Choice A) is correct: a perforating (Volkmann''s) canal is the channel that branches off from the central canal at right angles and houses the vessels and nerves that connect osteons and reach the bone surfaces. (Choice B) names the central (Haversian) canal, which runs longitudinally down the center of each osteon, not transversely between osteons, so it carries the vessel along the bone''s long axis rather than across it. (Choice C) names a canaliculus, one of the tiny channels that connect lacunae to one another and to the central canal to transport nutrients to osteocytes; these are far too small to house a blood vessel. (Choice D) names a lacuna, a small space that houses an osteocyte at the borders of adjacent lamellae, not a vascular channel at all. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the longitudinal-versus-transverse geometry of osteon canals to identify which structure a transversely oriented vessel occupies.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
