-- Q9 [Bone Matrix & Microstructure] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Bone Matrix & Microstructure', 'In a region of compact bone, mineral deposition seals off the canaliculi that radiate from a single lacuna, while the surrounding central canal and its blood vessels remain intact. What is the most likely consequence for the osteocyte residing in that lacuna?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "It divides to replace neighboring lost osteocytes"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It differentiates into an osteoclast to resorb the matrix"}, {"label": "C", "text": "It sustains itself by diffusion through the calcified matrix"}, {"label": "D", "text": "It dies once it can no longer receive nutrients"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because canaliculi are the only conduits delivering nutrients from the central canal''s vessels to an osteocyte sealed within solid matrix, so obstructing them starves the cell. Osteocytes are entrapped within calcified bone matrix and "communicate with each other and receive nutrients via long cytoplasmic processes that extend through canaliculi" (lines 9582-9583); the canaliculi "connect with the canaliculi of other lacunae and eventually with the central canal," and this system is what "allows nutrients to be transported to the osteocytes and wastes to be removed from them" (lines 9657-9658). Because the matrix is calcified and rigid rather than fluid, no alternative diffusion pathway exists, so loss of the canalicular network cuts off the osteocyte''s sole supply line and the deeply embedded cell cannot survive. The intact central canal vessels are irrelevant once the channels linking them to the lacuna are blocked. (Choice A) is incorrect: osteocytes "lack mitotic activity" (line 9581), so the cell cannot divide; only osteogenic cells divide to replenish lost bone cells (lines 9587-9588). (Choice B) is incorrect: osteoclasts "originate from monocytes and macrophages" (line 9592), not from osteocytes, so an entrapped osteocyte cannot convert into a resorptive cell. (Choice C) is incorrect: the matrix is calcified and solid, and the text presents canaliculi as the route for nutrient transport precisely because direct diffusion through the mineralized matrix is not viable (lines 9657-9658); this choice is tempting because diffusion does supply cells in soft tissues, but it fails in rigid bone. (Choice D) is correct: with its only nutrient channels obstructed, the embedded osteocyte loses access to the central canal''s blood supply and dies. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the structural fact that solid matrix permits nutrient delivery only through canaliculi to predict the outcome of obstructing that pathway.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
