-- Q11 [Bone Remodeling & Calcium Homeostasis] easy skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Bone Remodeling & Calcium Homeostasis', 'After a fracture heals, a region of newly deposited bone matrix requires the synthesis and secretion of fresh collagen and calcium salts to rebuild the damaged area. The cell most directly responsible for laying down this new bone matrix is the:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "osteocyte"}, {"label": "B", "text": "osteoclast"}, {"label": "C", "text": "osteoblast"}, {"label": "D", "text": "osteogenic cell"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because the osteoblast is the bone cell that synthesizes and secretes the collagen matrix and calcium salts of new bone. The textbook states directly that "The osteoblast is the bone cell responsible for forming new bone" and that osteoblasts "synthesize and secrete the collagen matrix and calcium salts" (lines 9576–9578). During fracture repair this matrix-depositing activity rebuilds the damaged region, after which the osteoblast can become entrapped in the calcified matrix and convert into an osteocyte. (Choice A) The osteocyte is the mature bone cell entrapped in lacunae that "maintain[s] the mineral concentration of the matrix" (lines 9579–9581); it preserves existing matrix rather than secreting new bone matrix. (Choice B) The osteoclast is "the cell responsible for bone resorption, or breakdown" (line 9591); it dissolves matrix rather than building it, the opposite of the action described. (Choice C) is correct: only the osteoblast actively synthesizes and secretes the collagen and calcium-salt matrix that constitutes new bone, making it the formation cell. (Choice D) The osteogenic cell is the undifferentiated dividing precursor that "differentiate[s] and develop[s] into osteoblasts" (lines 9586–9588); it gives rise to the matrix-secreting cell but does not itself deposit bone matrix. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the specific function assigned to each of the four bone cell types.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 70)
  RETURNING id
)
