-- Q12 [Bone Remodeling & Calcium Homeostasis] medium skill2 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Bone Remodeling & Calcium Homeostasis', 'A collegiate rower begins a year-round resistance-training program that sustains a markedly higher mechanical load on the long bones of the limbs. Compared with the rower''s previous training year, the bones of the loaded limbs are most likely to show which change?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Increased density from net mineral deposition by osteoblasts"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Decreased density from net mineral resorption by osteoclasts"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Decreased density from osteoclast-driven calcium release into blood"}, {"label": "D", "text": "No density change because remodeling only maintains existing mass"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because sustained mechanical loading stimulates the deposition of mineral salts and collagen fibers, shifting the remodeling balance toward osteoblast-driven net bone formation and greater density. The textbook states that "mechanical stress stimulates the deposition of mineral salts and collagen fibers" and that "the internal and external structure of a bone will change as stress increases or decreases so that the bone is an ideal size and weight for the amount of activity it endures" (Anatomy & Physiology 2e, lines 10067-10069). It further notes that "people who exercise regularly have greater bone density than those who are more sedentary" and that "resistance training has a greater effect than cardiovascular activities" (lines 10074-10076). Because osteoblasts are the cells responsible for bone formation (line 9576) and osteoclasts for resorption (line 9591), increased load tips the ongoing osteoblast/osteoclast balance toward net deposition. (Choice A) is correct: the adaptive response to higher mechanical load is net osteoblastic deposition of mineral salts, which raises bone density specifically in the loaded limbs. (Choice B) reverses the direction of the response; net resorption and loss of "mineral salts and collagen fibers, and thus strength" occur with a LACK of mechanical stress, as during spaceflight, not with increased loading (lines 10064-10067). (Choice C) confuses mechanically driven remodeling with the calcium-homeostasis pathway in which parathyroid hormone "stimulates osteoclast proliferation and activity" so that "calcium is released from the bones into the" blood (lines 10220-10221); that hormonal pathway, not mechanical loading, drives osteoclastic calcium release. (Choice D) is a misconception that ignores adaptive remodeling; remodeling is not merely maintenance, because "the internal and external structure of a bone will change as stress increases or decreases," producing thicker, denser bone under greater load (lines 10068-10071). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that mechanical stress drives osteoblastic deposition to predict the density change in a specific loaded-limb scenario.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
