-- Q13 [Bone Remodeling & Calcium Homeostasis] hard skill4 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Bone Remodeling & Calcium Homeostasis', 'A clinician measures blood calcium and parathyroid hormone (PTH) in three patients:

| Patient | Blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) | Plasma PTH |
|---------|--------------------------|------------|
| 1       | Low                      | High       |
| 2       | Normal                   | Normal     |
| 3       | High                     | Low        |

Based on these data, the elevated PTH in Patient 1 most directly restores blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) by acting on bone in which way?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Inhibiting osteoclasts, lowering blood \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Stimulating osteoblasts to deposit bone matrix"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Stimulating osteoclasts, lowering blood \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Stimulating osteoclasts, releasing \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) into blood"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is D because PTH stimulates osteoclast proliferation and activity, which resorbs bone and releases calcium into the circulation, raising blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\). The data show an inverse relationship: Patient 1''s low blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is paired with high PTH, because cells of the parathyroid gland release PTH when calcium is not binding their plasma-membrane receptors. The textbook states "PTH stimulates osteoclast proliferation and activity. As a result, calcium is released from the bones into the circulation, thus increasing the calcium ion concentration in the blood" (lines 10220-10221), and that this "demineralization process releases calcium into the blood" (line 10303). Thus the elevated PTH in Patient 1 drives osteoclast-mediated resorption to correct the low blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\). (Choice A) is incorrect: inhibiting osteoclasts and lowering blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) describes calcitonin, which "inhibits osteoclast activity and stimulates calcium uptake by the bones, thus reducing the concentration of calcium ions in the blood" (lines 10235-10236), not PTH. (Choice B) is incorrect: PTH does not stimulate bone deposition; it "also inhibits osteoblasts, the cells involved in bone deposition, thereby sparing blood calcium" (line 32253), so depositing matrix would lower, not raise, blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\). (Choice C) is incorrect: although PTH does stimulate osteoclasts, osteoclast resorption releases calcium and raises blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\); lowering blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) reverses the actual outcome, since "calcium is released from the bones into the circulation, thus increasing the calcium ion concentration in the blood" (lines 10220-10221). (Choice D) is correct: PTH stimulates osteoclasts to resorb bone and release \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) into the blood, which is exactly the corrective response to Patient 1''s low blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) and consistent with the inverse table relationship. This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the inverse blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-versus-PTH pattern in the table to infer PTH''s direction of action on bone.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 160)
  RETURNING id
)
