|---------|--------------------|--------------------------|
| X | Increased | Rises |
| Y | Decreased | Falls |

If Hormone Y is calcitonin, the physiological stimulus that normally triggers its secretion most likely produces which response?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Low blood \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) triggering bone resorption"}, {"label": "B", "text": "High blood \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) triggering bone calcium uptake"}, {"label": "C", "text": "High blood \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) triggering bone resorption"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Low blood \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\) triggering bone calcium uptake"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because calcitonin is secreted in response to elevated blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) and lowers it by inhibiting osteoclasts and driving calcium uptake into bone. In the table, Hormone Y decreases osteoclast activity and lowers blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\), matching calcitonin: "Calcitonin inhibits osteoclast activity and stimulates calcium uptake by the bones, thus reducing the concentration of calcium ions in the blood" (A&P 2e, lines 10235-10236). Its secretion is triggered by the opposite of its effect, completing a negative-feedback loop: "When blood levels of calcium get too high, the thyroid gland is stimulated to release calcitonin ... All of these actions lower blood levels of calcium" (A&P 2e, lines 10310-10312). Thus the normal stimulus (high blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)) produces calcium uptake by bone. (Choice A) is incorrect because low blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is the stimulus for PTH (Hormone X), and bone resorption raises calcium rather than lowering it (lines 10220-10221); this pairs the wrong stimulus with the wrong bone effect. (Choice B) is correct: calcitonin is released when blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is high and stimulates calcium uptake by bone, which lowers blood calcium back toward normal, the action recorded for Hormone Y. (Choice C) is incorrect because, although high blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is indeed calcitonin''s correct stimulus, calcitonin inhibits rather than promotes osteoclast-driven resorption; resorption would raise calcium, contradicting the falling \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) of Hormone Y (lines 10235-10236). (Choice D) is incorrect because it reverses the feedback trigger: calcitonin''s correct bone effect (calcium uptake) is paired with low blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\), but calcitonin is secreted when calcium is high, not low (line 10310). This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the tabulated osteoclast activity and blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) changes to match Hormone Y to calcitonin and infer the negative-feedback stimulus that drives its secretion.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 160)
  RETURNING id
)
