-- Q15 [Bone Remodeling & Calcium Homeostasis] hard skill4 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Bone Remodeling & Calcium Homeostasis', 'A patient is diagnosed with a tumor that chronically secretes a peptide that binds and activates the same receptors as parathyroid hormone. A clinician records the resulting changes in osteoclast activity at the patient''s bone surfaces, as shown.

| Variable | Observed change |
|---|---|
| PTH-receptor signaling | Persistently elevated |
| Osteoclast proliferation and activity | Increased |
| Osteoblast-mediated bone deposition | Outpaced by resorption |

Based on these data, the patient is most likely to present with which combined skeletal and blood finding?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Increased bone density with decreased blood \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Decreased bone density with decreased blood \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Decreased bone density with elevated blood \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Increased bone density with elevated blood \\(\\text{Ca}^{2+}\\)"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because chronic PTH-like signaling drives osteoclast-mediated resorption that strips mineral from bone and dumps \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) into the circulation, lowering bone density while raising blood calcium. PTH stimulates osteoclast proliferation and activity, so calcium is released from the bones into the circulation, thus increasing the calcium ion concentration in the blood (lines 10220-10221). When this PTH activity becomes excessive, as in hyperparathyroidism caused by overproduction of PTH, the result is excessive calcium reabsorption from bone that can significantly decrease bone density, leading to spontaneous fractures or deformities, while blood calcium levels rise (lines 10220-10221, 32273-32275). The data table''s persistently elevated PTH-receptor signaling and resorption outpacing deposition map directly onto these two coupled outcomes. (Choice A) is incorrect because increased density with falling blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) describes the action of calcitonin, which inhibits osteoclast activity and stimulates calcium uptake by bones, thus reducing the concentration of calcium ions in the blood (lines 10235-10236, 10258); this reverses the relationship the table reports. (Choice B) is incorrect because, although decreased bone density correctly follows from osteoclast resorption, the calcium liberated from the demineralized matrix enters the circulation and raises blood \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) rather than lowering it (lines 10220-10221); the resorbed mineral does not disappear from the blood. (Choice C) is correct: persistent PTH-receptor activation increases osteoclast resorption, demineralizing bone (lower density) and releasing that calcium into blood (elevated \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)), exactly the hyperparathyroidism pattern of decreased bone density with rising blood calcium (lines 32274-32275). (Choice D) is incorrect because increased bone density would require net deposition by osteoblasts, the cells responsible for forming new bone, whereas PTH stimulates osteoclasts that resorb bone, so density falls rather than rises (lines 9591, 10255, 32252-32253). This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the tabulated hormone-perturbation changes and reason from them to the combined bone-density and blood-calcium prediction.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 160)
  RETURNING id
