-- Q16 [Bone Remodeling & Calcium Homeostasis] hard skill4 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Bone Remodeling & Calcium Homeostasis', 'A clinic measures serum vitamin D (calcitriol) status and the degree of bone matrix mineralization in several patients, summarized below.

| Patient | Vitamin D (calcitriol) status | Intestinal \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) absorption | Bone mineralization |
|---------|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------|---------------------|
| W | Normal | Normal | Fully mineralized |
| X | Normal | Normal | Fully mineralized |
| Y | Severely deficient | Markedly reduced | ? |

Given that the bone matrix is built from hydroxyapatite \(\big(\text{Ca}_{10}(\text{PO}_4)_6(\text{OH})_2\big)\), which outcome for Patient Y is most consistent with the data?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Soft, under-mineralized bone with elevated osteoclast resorption"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Soft, under-mineralized bone with suppressed osteoclast resorption"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Denser, over-mineralized bone with elevated osteoclast resorption"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Fully mineralized bone with suppressed calcitonin release"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because vitamin D deficiency reduces intestinal \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) absorption, lowering available calcium so that bone is poorly mineralized while the resulting low blood calcium drives compensatory parathyroid-hormone-mediated osteoclast resorption. Calcium "cannot be absorbed from the small intestine without vitamin D" (lines 10085-10086), and calcitriol "Stimulates absorption of calcium and phosphate from digestive tract" (line 10253), so Patient Y''s markedly reduced absorption starves the hydroxyapatite-forming process and yields the "softening of the bones" of osteomalacia or the calcium-deficient misshapen bone of rickets (lines 8550-8557). Because the parathyroid cells release PTH "when calcium is not binding to these receptors," and PTH "stimulates osteoclast proliferation and resorption of bone by osteoclasts," low blood calcium simultaneously increases osteoclast activity to pull \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) from the skeleton (lines 10301-10303). (Choice A) is correct: under-mineralized, softened bone coexists with elevated osteoclast resorption because the same hypocalcemia that prevents mineralization also triggers compensatory PTH-driven demineralization. (Choice B) keeps the correct softened, under-mineralized bone but reverses the resorption direction; hypocalcemia raises, not suppresses, osteoclast activity via PTH (lines 10301-10303). (Choice C) correctly notes elevated resorption but wrongly predicts denser, over-mineralized bone; deficient calcium absorption cannot build extra hydroxyapatite, and the text ties deficiency to softened, calcium-poor bone (lines 8550-8557). (Choice D) wrongly predicts full mineralization despite markedly reduced \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) absorption and misattributes the response to suppressed calcitonin, whereas the deficiency-driven pathway operates through PTH and calcitriol, not calcitonin (lines 10298-10305). This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the table of vitamin D status and calcium absorption to predict the mineralization and resorption outcome.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 4, 165)
  RETURNING id
)
