-- Q18 [Cartilage & Ossification] medium skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Cartilage & Ossification', 'A runner tears the hyaline articular cartilage covering a knee joint, and months later imaging shows the lesion has barely repaired, whereas a fracture in the adjacent bone heals fully within weeks. The slow repair of the cartilage is most directly explained by which property of cartilage relative to bone?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Its chondrocytes are confined within lacunae"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It is avascular, so exchange occurs only by diffusion"}, {"label": "C", "text": "It is densely innervated but poorly oxygenated"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Its matrix is mineralized with calcium phosphate"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because cartilage has no blood vessels, so nutrients reach chondrocytes and wastes leave only by slow diffusion through the matrix, which limits the repair response. Unlike most connective tissues, cartilage is avascular, meaning it has no blood vessels supplying nutrients and removing metabolic wastes, and all of these functions are carried on by diffusion through the matrix; this is why damaged cartilage does not repair itself as readily as most tissues do. Bone, by contrast, is a highly vascularized tissue, and unlike cartilage, bone tissue can recover from injuries in a relatively short time because its blood supply delivers oxygen, nutrients, and repair cells directly to the injury site. The contrast in the scenario therefore traces directly to the presence versus absence of a vascular supply. (Choice A) Chondrocytes do sit within lacunae, but bone cells (osteocytes) likewise occupy lacunae, so confinement in lacunae is shared by both tissues and cannot explain why one heals faster than the other. (Choice B) is correct: cartilage is avascular and relies on diffusion through the matrix for all nutrient and waste exchange, which is the textbook reason damaged cartilage heals far more slowly than vascularized bone. (Choice C) Cartilage is not densely innervated; the limiting factor described in the text is the absence of blood vessels and reliance on diffusion, not a nerve supply, so innervation is not the operative property here. (Choice D) Mineralization with calcium phosphate (hydroxyapatite) is a property of bone, not of healthy hyaline cartilage, so attributing a mineralized matrix to the cartilage reverses the actual tissue chemistry. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the avascular, diffusion-dependent nature of cartilage to predict why a specific cartilage injury heals more slowly than a bone fracture.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
