-- Q21 [Joints & Movement] easy skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Joints & Movement', 'The coronal suture of an adult skull immobilizes the frontal bone against the right and left parietal bones, leaving no appreciable movement at the joint. Judging by its degree of mobility, this suture is most accurately placed in which functional joint category?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Diarthrosis"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Amphiarthrosis"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Synarthrosis"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Synovial joint"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because the functional classification of joints is based on the amount of movement permitted, and a skull suture allows essentially no movement, making it a synarthrosis. The textbook defines a synarthrosis as "an immobile or nearly immobile joint" whose strong union is important "at locations where the bones provide protection for internal organs," and it explicitly lists "sutures, the fibrous joints between the bones of the skull that surround and protect the brain" as an example. The figure caption reinforces this, stating that "The suture joints of the skull are an example of a synarthrosis, an immobile or essentially immobile joint." Functional categories are assigned strictly by mobility, so the immovable suture falls into the synarthrosis class regardless of its fibrous structure. (Choice A) Diarthrosis is incorrect because a diarthrosis "is a freely moveable joint" that includes "all synovial joints of the body"; this describes the opposite mobility extreme from an immovable suture. (Choice B) Amphiarthrosis is incorrect because an amphiarthrosis is "a joint that has limited mobility," such as the cartilaginous intervertebral joints, whereas a suture permits essentially no movement rather than limited movement. (Choice C) is correct: because the functional classification is "determined by the amount of mobility found between the adjacent bones," and a suture is immobile, it is a synarthrosis, the category the text directly assigns to skull sutures. (Choice D) Synovial joint is incorrect because "synovial" is a structural classification defined by the presence of a fluid-filled joint cavity, and all synovial joints are functionally classified as diarthroses; a suture has no joint cavity and is a fibrous joint, so this label confuses the structural category with the requested functional one. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the functional classification scheme for joints and apply the definition of a synarthrosis to a skull suture.', 'easy', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
