-- Q23 [Joints & Movement] medium skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Joints & Movement', 'During flexion of the knee, the femur and tibia move smoothly against each other within the joint cavity without their bony surfaces grinding. Which structure most directly reduces friction between the two articulating bone surfaces themselves?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Articular cartilage covering each bone end"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Synovial fluid filling the joint cavity"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Synovial membrane lining the articular capsule"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A bursa adjacent to the joint"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is A because articular cartilage is the thin hyaline-cartilage layer that covers each articulating bone surface and directly prevents the bones from grinding against each other. The textbook states that friction between the bones at a synovial joint is prevented by the presence of the articular cartilage, a thin layer of hyaline cartilage that covers the entire articulating surface of each bone, and that this cartilage acts like a Teflon coating over the bone surface, allowing the articulating bones to move smoothly against each other without damaging the underlying bone tissue. Because the cartilage physically covers the bone ends, it is the structure in direct contact at the articulating surfaces, making it the most direct friction-reducer between the bones. (Choice A) is correct: the articular cartilage on each bone is not continuous with the cartilage of the opposing bone, so the two smooth caps slide against each other within the cavity. (Choice B) Synovial fluid is a real component that provides lubrication to further reduce friction between the bones, but the textbook frames it as a supplemental lubricant within the cavity rather than the structure that covers the bone ends; the phrase ''further reduce'' signals it acts in addition to the cartilage that directly covers the surfaces. (Choice C) The synovial membrane lines the inner surface of the articular capsule and secretes synovial fluid, so it produces the lubricant but does not itself contact or cover the articulating bone surfaces. (Choice D) A bursa is a fluid-filled sac that reduces friction by separating skin, ligaments, muscles, or tendons that rub near a joint; it lies outside the joint and acts on soft tissues, not on the bone-to-bone articulating surfaces. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall which specific synovial-joint structure directly covers the bone ends to reduce friction between articulating surfaces.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 1, 100)
  RETURNING id
)
