-- Q24 [Joints & Movement] hard skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Joints & Movement', 'A person lifts a cup by bending the elbow: the biceps brachii contracts as the prime mover, the brachialis assists, and the triceps brachii opposes the motion. For this specific flexion of the forearm, which classification is most accurate?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The brachialis is acting as the antagonist of flexion"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The biceps brachii origin is on the moving forearm bone"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The biceps brachii insertion is on the moving forearm bone"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The triceps brachii is acting as a synergist of flexion"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is C because the insertion is the muscle''s moveable end, which attaches to the bone being pulled—here, the forearm that swings up during flexion. During forearm flexion the biceps brachii pulls the forearm toward the body, so its insertion lies on the moving forearm bone while its origin attaches to the fixed, stabilized bone of the shoulder girdle; the textbook states that "the moveable end of the muscle that attaches to the bone being pulled is called the muscle''s insertion, and the end of the muscle attached to a fixed (stabilized) bone is called the origin" (lines 18803–18805). The brachialis assists the prime mover and is therefore a synergist, whereas the triceps brachii performs the opposite action and is the antagonist (lines 18808–18809, 18818, 18845–18846). (Choice A) The brachialis assists the biceps in flexion, so it is a synergist, not an antagonist; an antagonist is "a muscle with the opposite action of the prime mover" (line 18818), which describes the triceps instead. (Choice B) This reverses origin and insertion: the origin is the end attached to the fixed bone, not the moving forearm bone (lines 18804–18805). (Choice C) is correct: the insertion is the moveable end attached to the bone being pulled, and during elbow flexion the forearm is the bone that moves. (Choice D) The triceps brachii extends the forearm, the opposite action, so it is the antagonist, not a synergist; a synergist is a "muscle whose contraction helps a prime mover in an action" (line 20571). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of origin, insertion, synergist, and antagonist to a specific elbow-flexion scenario rather than recall them in isolation.', 'hard', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
