-- Q25 [Joints & Movement] medium skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'The Musculoskeletal System', 'Joints & Movement', 'Standing in anatomical position, a person keeps the elbow straight and raises one upper limb laterally out to the side, moving it away from the trunk until the arm is horizontal. This action at the shoulder joint is termed:', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Adduction"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Abduction"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Flexion"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Lateral rotation"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Organ Systems.'' The answer is B because moving the limb laterally away from the midline of the body is, by definition, abduction. Abduction and adduction occur within the coronal plane and involve medial-lateral motions of the limbs; the textbook states that abduction "moves the limb laterally away from the midline of the body" and gives the specific example that "abduction is raising the arm at the shoulder joint, moving it laterally away from the body" (openstax-anatomy-physiology-2e.txt, lines 15504-15506). The shoulder is a ball-and-socket joint at which abduction is permitted. (Choice A) Adduction is the opposing movement that "brings the limb toward the body or across the midline" (lines 15504-15506); it describes lowering the raised arm back to the side, the reverse of the action described. (Choice B) is correct: raising the straight limb laterally away from the trunk in the coronal plane is abduction at the shoulder. (Choice C) Flexion takes place within the sagittal plane and "decreases the angle between the bones," with anterior-going arm motion at the shoulder being flexion (lines 15472, 15485-15487); raising the arm sideways in the coronal plane is not flexion. (Choice D) Lateral (external) rotation turns the humerus around its long axis so the anterior surface moves away from the midline (lines 15530-15534); it spins the bone in place rather than swinging the whole limb away from the trunk. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of the body-movement terms to a specific described limb action and select the matching term.', 'medium', '3B', 'Organ Systems', 'biology', 2, 100)
  RETURNING id
)
